\begin_layout Abstract
The effects of income inequality on motivation remain debated amid stagnant mobility.
 Although status is central to inequality discussions,
 it is rarely endogenized in models,
 limiting insights into dynamic interactions.
 This paper presents a game-theoretic model combining innate ability and social capital in small-scale status tournaments,
 where investments signal traits while enhancing productivity.
 In a dynamic hierarchy of high- and low-status positions,
 relative income and participation costs drive outcomes.
 Moderate inequality stimulates investments,
 but excess renders tournaments unsustainable;
 costs rise endogenously with competition;
 and only two stable equilibria emerge—
universal or elite-only participation.
 These results highlight how signaling sustains inequality traps,
 extending static status models to probabilistic settings.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mechanisms through which relative income shapes participation are contested.
 Some view inequality as an incentive rewarding effort and mobility 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Baron2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 while others see it discouraging long-term productivity 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Gesiarz2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Static treatments of status obscure these dynamics:
 investments respond to both subjective (perceived rank) and objective (structural) positions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wegener1992"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper endogenizes status in a model of social capital investments,
 incorporating signaling via networks and affiliations.
 Agents compete in probabilistic tournaments for high-status positions (income 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}>y_{1}$
\end_inset

),
 blending raw talent (
\begin_inset Formula $r\sim U[0,1]$
\end_inset

) and acquired capital (
\begin_inset Formula $s\sim U[0,1]$
\end_inset

,
 weighted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu<1/2$
\end_inset

 for signaling dominance).
 Success depends on income gaps (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y=y_{2}-y_{1}$
\end_inset

) and participation costs (
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

),
 with reference-dependent utility capturing local contentment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Three results emerge:
 (1) Moderate 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y$
\end_inset

 boosts investments,
 but high 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y/\nu$
\end_inset

 makes tournaments unsustainable;
 (2) Competition endogenously raises 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

,
 limiting mobility;
 (3) Two stable Nash equilibria:
 universal (low 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu/\Delta y$
\end_inset

) or elite-only (high 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu/\Delta y$
\end_inset

).
 These clarify opportunity-outcome tensions,
 with welfare favoring universal under low costs.
\end_layout

