\begin_layout Section
Literature Survey
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inequality's motivational role spans economics and sociology.
 Early views emphasize incentives for effort 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "BeckerTomes1979,Baron2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 but recent work shows thresholds where it erodes trust and participation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "AlesinaPerotti1996,Stiglitz2012,Chetty2014"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Status complicates this:
 as a motivator 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Frank1993,BenabouTirole2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 it contrasts social vs.
 economic goals,
 hindering endogenization 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Goode1978"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Yet status is intertwined with rewards 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "MoldovanuSelaShi2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 shaping behavior via objective/subjective rank 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wegener1992,Postlewaite1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Tournament models capture this indirectly through relative ranking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Signaling theory 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Spence1973"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and job competition 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Thurow1975"
literal "false"

\end_inset

